{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"On node1, the datauser account is limited to 100 MB of storage under /srv/data by filesystem-level enforcement.","status":"satisfied","summary":"A dedicated XFS filesystem is mounted at /srv/data with user-quota accounting and enforcement enabled. datauser has matching 100M soft and hard limits. Direct tests before and after restart attempted a 110 MiB write as datauser and failed with “Disk quota exceeded” without exceeding the configured limit.","evidence_ids":["cmd-761dc2d1d9204508","cmd-f593af249d24430d","cmd-8341863fc2aa4baa","cmd-6af19aec5e834c1c"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Keep the installed operating-system release and kernel, using software compatible with the legacy platform.","status":"satisfied","summary":"The node remained on Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9.8 with kernel 5.14.0-687.20.1.el9_8 before and after the work and restart. The solution uses the installed RHEL 9 XFS tooling, and package consistency remained healthy.","evidence_ids":["global:before_prepare:node1:platform","cmd-92aaf619619a40ae","cmd-8341863fc2aa4baa","global:after_executor:node1:platform","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]}],"confidence":0.99,"operational_hygiene":{"score":1,"summary":"No unnecessary mutations were identified. Creating datauser, the mount point, sparse XFS backing file, persistent fstab entry, and quota configuration directly implemented the requested outcome (cmd-dfb65c82e2fd4eb1, cmd-761dc2d1d9204508). The quota-test files were bounded verification artifacts and were removed (cmd-f593af249d24430d, cmd-8341863fc2aa4baa). The retained persistence marker supported the mandatory restart recovery proof (cmd-92aaf619619a40ae). The reboot was required by the provider-wide evidence protocol (cmd-1f1e9dea0d7e4a62). Final observations show no failed services, new exposure, temporary residue, or damaged package state.","evidence_ids":["cmd-dfb65c82e2fd4eb1","cmd-761dc2d1d9204508","cmd-f593af249d24430d","cmd-92aaf619619a40ae","cmd-1f1e9dea0d7e4a62","cmd-8341863fc2aa4baa","cmd-6af19aec5e834c1c","global:after_executor:node1:failed-services","global:after_executor:node1:listening-sockets","global:after_executor:node1:temporary-files","global:after_executor:node1:package-health"]},"overall_summary":"All material task outcomes are satisfied. Filesystem-level XFS quota enforcement limits datauser to 100M under /srv/data, survives restart, and leaves the original RHEL release and kernel intact. The mandatory node-restart protocol is complete: healthy pre-restart state, acknowledged restart, changed boot identity, and recovered system, mount, quota, data, and enforcement were directly observed and independently corroborated by lifecycle boot identities.","limitations":["The lifecycle snapshots cover generic platform health rather than quota-specific state; quota configuration and behavior are established by direct executor command evidence.","The instruction uses “100 MB” while XFS reports the configured limit as “100M” and the tests interpret it as 100 MiB (104,857,600 bytes), a conventional filesystem-tool interpretation."]}
